---
import AgentView from "../components/AgentView.svelte";
import LineChart from "../components/LineChart.svelte";
import ReplayCompare from "../components/ReplayCompare.svelte";
import training from "../data/training.json";
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { AGENTS, RATING_GAMES } from "../lib/agents";

const v2 = training.two_fuse_v2.history;
const curves = [
  { id: "heuristic", label: "heuristic", color: "#ece6d6", dash: "" },
  { id: "camper", label: "camper", color: "#ece6d6", dash: "7 5" },
  { id: "wanderer", label: "wanderer", color: "#ece6d6", dash: "2 4" },
].map((s) => ({
  ...s,
  points: v2.map((row) => ({
    x: row.timesteps,
    y: (row as Record<string, number>)[`vs_${s.id}`]!,
    note: `${(row as Record<string, number>)[`steps_vs_${s.id}`]} steps a game`,
  })),
}));
const v1 = training.two_fuse_v1.history.map((row) => ({ x: row.timesteps, y: row.vs_heuristic }));

// 200 games per bot over arena_1, map_2 and map_3, greedy play (docs/EXPERIMENTS.md)
const results = [
  { bot: "Heuristic", v1: 0.52, v2: 0.76 },
  { bot: "Camper", v1: 0.42, v2: 0.77 },
  { bot: "Wanderer", v1: 0.43, v2: 0.69 },
];
const DW = 640;
const DX = (v: number) => 120 + v * (DW - 170);
const maxElo = Math.max(...AGENTS.map((a) => a.elo));
const minElo = Math.min(...AGENTS.map((a) => a.elo));
---

<Base
  title="How they learned · smart-bomberman"
  description="How Two-Fuse was trained: a warm start from a map-clearing agent, league self-play, a failure that only showed up in human play, and the fix."
  current="learn"
>
  <article class="story">
    <header class="opening">
      <h1>How they learned</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Every opponent on this site is either a short script or a neural network trained by playing. This is how the
        trained one, Two-Fuse, got good: where it started, what it sees, how it trained against copies of itself, and
        the failure that only showed up when a person played it.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="prose panel" aria-labelledby="start">
      <h2 id="start">It started by clearing maps, not fighting</h2>
      <p>
        The 2023 team project had a Bomberman game in pygame and several attempts at an agent. None could learn: the
        game only advanced while drawing frames, bomb fuses ran on wall-clock seconds, and one tile of movement took 16
        to 32 actions. The 2026 revival rebuilt the rules on a tile grid where one action moves one tile and every
        timer counts steps.
      </p>
      <p>
        On that grid a PPO agent (Stable-Baselines3, a 256×256 network) learned to clear levels: kill every wandering
        enemy, then reach the door. Over 200 episodes it cleared the maze map 91% of the time and the open map 79.5%.
        A random policy cleared neither.
      </p>
      <p>
        Battle mode needed a fighter, not a level clearer. Rather than start from nothing, its weights were copied
        into the larger Battle-mode network with the opponent standing in as "the enemy to hunt". The copy plays
        exactly like the original until training changes it. Out of the box it beat the attacking bot about 13% of
        the time.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="wide" aria-labelledby="sees">
      <div class="prose panel">
        <h2 id="sees">What it sees</h2>
        <p>
          Two-Fuse never sees the whole board. Each step it reads a 9×9 window centred on itself, as nine planes: walls,
          breakable walls, bombs, flames, danger, hostiles, an empty door plane left over from map clearing, the
          opponent, and cells about to close. Then 24 numbers: where the opponent is and how far, whether its bomb is
          ready, whether it could drop one and still escape, which neighbouring step leads to safety, how long until
          sudden death. A network of two 256-unit layers turns those 753 inputs into six probabilities.
        </p>
      </div>
      <AgentView client:visible />
    </section>

    <section class="prose panel" aria-labelledby="league">
      <h2 id="league">It trained against copies of itself</h2>
      <p>
        Two-Fuse trained by league self-play. Every 250,000 steps a snapshot of it joins the league. Each training match
        then faces the latest snapshot (40%), an older one (25%), or a scripted bot (35%), and starting corners are a
        coin flip. Older snapshots keep it from forgetting how to beat earlier versions of itself; bots keep it honest
        against styles no snapshot plays.
      </p>
      <p>
        Rewards are mostly about the result: +5 for a win, −5 for a loss, −2 for dying together, and a cost of 0.01
        for every step. Small extras pay for opening walls (+0.05), killing wandering enemies (+0.3), closing in to
        three tiles of the opponent, and dropping a bomb near it with a way out (+0.2). At the end, a round robin
        between the last eight snapshots and the bots, fitted to Elo, picks the champion.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="wide" aria-labelledby="curve">
      <LineChart
        client:visible
        series={curves}
        title="Two-Fuse v2's win rate against each bot during training, 30 games per bot at each check"
      />
      <p class="aside">
        Each point is only 30 games, so single points swing by ±15%. Hover a point for how long those games took. v1 was
        only ever checked against the heuristic bot; it ended at {Math.round(v1[v1.length - 1]!.y * 100)}% after 5M steps.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="wide" aria-labelledby="failure">
      <div class="prose panel">
        <h2 id="failure">The failure only a person found</h2>
        <p>
          The first champion, v1, beat the heuristic bot 66% of the time over 200 games on the original, denser arena. Then a person played it on hard
          and reported that it never bombed. It walked left, right, left, right.
        </p>
        <p>
          The scoreboard had hidden it. Every opponent v1 ever met walked towards it: the heuristic bot attacks, and so
          do its own snapshots. It had learned to wait for the opponent to arrive, and a person who held back left it
          waiting forever, until sudden death closed the arena. The replay below is the real v1 and v2 networks against
          an opponent that never moves.
        </p>
      </div>
      <ReplayCompare client:visible />
    </section>

    <section class="prose panel" aria-labelledby="fix">
      <h2 id="fix">What changed in v2</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Opponents that do not come to you.</strong> A camper that only dodges and a wanderer that walks at random.
          Neither ever bombs; together they are 40% of the bot matches.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Waiting stopped paying.</strong> The step cost doubled to 0.01, and a win before sudden death earns an
          extra +2.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>A thinner arena.</strong> arena_1 went from 70 breakable walls to 28, so fighters meet before the walls
          close.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Fair sudden death.</strong> Each closing cell's mirror closes right after it, so neither corner is
          squeezed first.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Checks against all three bots</strong> during training, with match length logged, so passive play
          shows up as long matches.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>v2 continued from v1 for another 6M steps. The 4.5M-step snapshot is the one you play.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="wide" id="results" aria-labelledby="results-title">
      <div class="prose panel">
        <h2 id="results-title">Results</h2>
        <p>
          200 games per bot, spread over arena_1, map_2 and map_3 with both starting corners, Two-Fuse playing its best
          move each step.
        </p>
      </div>
      <figure class="dumbbell panel">
        <figcaption>Win rate, v1 → v2</figcaption>
        <svg viewBox={`0 0 ${DW} 170`} role="img" aria-label="Win rate of v1 and v2 against each bot">
          {
            [0, 0.25, 0.5, 0.75, 1].map((t) => (
              <g>
                <line x1={DX(t)} x2={DX(t)} y1="18" y2="140" class="grid" />
                <text x={DX(t)} y="160" class="tick" text-anchor="middle">{`${t * 100}%`}</text>
              </g>
            ))
          }
          {
            results.map((r, i) => (
              <g>
                <text x="0" y={40 + i * 40} class="label" dominant-baseline="middle">{r.bot}</text>
                <line x1={DX(r.v1)} x2={DX(r.v2)} y1={40 + i * 40} y2={40 + i * 40} class="link" />
                <circle cx={DX(r.v1)} cy={40 + i * 40} r="6" class="v1">
                  <title>{`v1 vs ${r.bot.toLowerCase()}: ${Math.round(r.v1 * 100)}%`}</title>
                </circle>
                <circle cx={DX(r.v2)} cy={40 + i * 40} r="6" class="v2">
                  <title>{`v2 vs ${r.bot.toLowerCase()}: ${Math.round(r.v2 * 100)}%`}</title>
                </circle>
                <text x={DX(r.v2) + 12} y={40 + i * 40} class="value" dominant-baseline="middle">{`${Math.round(r.v2 * 100)}%`}</text>
              </g>
            ))
          }
        </svg>
        <p class="key"><span><i class="k1"></i>v1</span><span><i class="k2"></i>v2</span></p>
      </figure>
      <div class="tables">
        <table class="panel">
          <caption>Against an opponent that never moves, 30 games</caption>
          <thead><tr><th scope="col"></th><th scope="col">v1</th><th scope="col">v2</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row">Bombed it</th><td>4</td><td>19</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Won before sudden death</th><td>20%</td><td>50%</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Bombs per game</th><td>5.5</td><td>11.6</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <table class="panel">
          <caption>Elo from a round robin, {RATING_GAMES} games per pair</caption>
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Agent</th><th scope="col">Elo</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              AGENTS.slice()
                .reverse()
                .map((a) => (
                  <tr>
                    <th scope="row">{a.name}</th>
                    <td>{a.elo}</td>
                  </tr>
                ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <div class="prose panel caveats">
        <h3>What is still weak</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>
            Against a passive opponent, about half the games still reach sudden death. On arena_1 in particular, against
            an opponent that never moves, Two-Fuse usually still waits for the walls.
          </li>
          <li>It sometimes walks into wandering enemies.</li>
          <li>
            The champion tournament plays only 12 games per pair and picked a slightly weaker snapshot (4.75M) than the
            one installed (4.5M); 200-game duels made the final call.
          </li>
          <li>
            Agent ratings span {minElo} to {maxElo}. Ratings only mean something relative to each other: players start at
            1000, the middle of that range.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .story {
    max-width: 1100px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 48px var(--gutter) 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: 40px;
  }
  .opening {
    display: grid;
    gap: 18px;
    max-width: 52rem;
  }
  h1 {
    font-size: clamp(2rem, 5.6vw, 4.2rem);
  }
  .lede {
    font-size: clamp(1.15rem, 1.6vw, 1.35rem);
    color: var(--text);
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }
  .prose {
    padding: clamp(22px, 3vw, 40px);
    max-width: calc(var(--measure) + 80px);
    display: grid;
    gap: 14px;
    font-size: 1.06rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
  }
  .prose h2 {
    font-size: clamp(1.3rem, 2.2vw, 1.75rem);
    margin-bottom: 4px;
  }
  .prose h3 {
    font-size: 1.1rem;
  }
  .prose ul {
    margin: 0;
    padding-left: 1.1em;
    display: grid;
    gap: 10px;
  }
  .wide {
    display: grid;
    gap: 18px;
  }
  .aside {
    color: var(--text-dim);
    font-size: 0.92rem;
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }
  .dumbbell {
    margin: 0;
    padding: 20px 22px;
  }
  .dumbbell figcaption {
    font-weight: 700;
  }
  .dumbbell svg {
    width: 100%;
    max-width: 760px;
    height: auto;
    display: block;
    margin-top: 6px;
  }
  .grid {
    stroke: #3d3d50;
  }
  .tick {
    font-size: 12px;
    fill: var(--text-faint);
  }
  .label {
    font-size: 14px;
    font-weight: 600;
    fill: var(--text);
  }
  .value {
    font-size: 13px;
    font-weight: 700;
    fill: var(--text);
  }
  .link {
    stroke: var(--line);
    stroke-width: 2;
  }
  .v1 {
    fill: #7c7c92;
    stroke: var(--panel);
    stroke-width: 2;
  }
  .v2 {
    fill: var(--two-fuse);
    stroke: var(--panel);
    stroke-width: 2;
  }
  .key {
    display: flex;
    gap: 8px 16px;
    align-items: center;
    font-size: 0.88rem;
    color: var(--text-dim);
  }
  .key i {
    width: 10px;
    height: 10px;
    border-radius: 50%;
    display: inline-block;
    margin-right: 6px;
    vertical-align: -1px;
  }
  .k1 {
    background: #7c7c92;
  }
  .k2 {
    background: var(--two-fuse);
  }
  .tables {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(280px, 1fr));
    gap: 18px;
    align-items: start;
  }
  table {
    width: 100%;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }
  caption {
    text-align: left;
    font-weight: 700;
    padding: 16px 18px 8px;
    background: var(--panel);
    color: var(--text);
  }
  th,
  td {
    padding: 8px 18px;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
    text-align: right;
  }
  th[scope="row"] {
    text-align: left;
    font-weight: 600;
  }
  thead th {
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--text-dim);
    border-bottom: 3px solid var(--edge);
  }
  .caveats {
    max-width: none;
  }
</style>
